  "client_view_text": " - Quora\nA write-ahead log, also known as a redo log or transaction log, is a data structure used by databases to ensure the consistency and integrity of data in the event of a failure or crash. Here\u2019s how it helps a database recover after a crash:\nThe database maintains a write-ahead log, which is a record of all changes made to the database before they are written to disk. When a database transaction is executed, the database will first write a record of the transaction to the write-ahead log before actually committing the changes to the database. This ensures that all changes are recorded in the log before they are written to disk. In case of a crash, the database can use the write-ahead log to recover the changes that were made before the crash.\nThe database can recover by replaying the transactions recorded in the write-ahead log. This means that the database will re-execute all the transactions that were written to the log before the crash, effectively undoing any incomplete or uncommitted changes. Once the replay is complete, the database will have recovered to a consistent state, reflecting the state of the system before the crash.\nIn summary, a write-ahead log helps a database recover after a crash by maintaining a record of all changes made to the database, allowing the"
